<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # All About My Body – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> All About My Body</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> Hats and Shoes!</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Locate people, objects, and things around them. Interactive: Respond to simple verbal commands in classroom settings.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can locate and identify people, objects, and things around them by describing body parts or clothing (e.g., "the boy with the blue hat" or "the red shoes"). Can follow and respond to simple commands in a game, calling out familiar body parts (e.g., "Touch your head!"). Can ask and respond to questions about familiar body parts and clothing using descriptive language (e.g., The teacher asks, "What is on your feet?" and the student responds, "These are shoes.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Simon Says" game. The teacher says commands using body parts and clothing vocabulary. Students follow only if "Simon Says" is included. This activates prior knowledge and focuses on listening and responding orally. (e.g., "Simon says, touch your head!", "Simon says, point to your toes!", "Simon says, put on your hat!").</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher explicitly models the target language for describing body parts and clothing items using realia or large flashcards. "This is my hand." (pointing to own hand, emphasizing /h/ sound). "These are my shoes." (pointing to own shoes, emphasizing /s/ sound). The teacher also models imperative commands: "Touch your nose." and WH-questions: "Where is your hat?" with clear pronunciation.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding using simple questions and gestures.</p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a picture of a hat: "Is this a shoe?" (Expected student response: "No, it's a hat.").</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to their foot: "Is this my hand?" (Expected student response: "No, it's your foot.").</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "All About My Body".</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple illustrated picture story or comic strip featuring two children (e.g., 'Leo' and 'Mia') getting dressed or playing, highlighting their body parts and clothing items. The register is informal and the format is a short narrative with dialogue bubbles.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Identifying body parts and clothing; following and giving simple instructions related to objects and location.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher displays the picture story. First, the teacher reads aloud, pointing to each character and item.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "Who is in the story?" (Leo and Mia). "What are they doing?" (Getting dressed/Playing).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> The teacher asks students to point to specific items or body parts in the story. "Point to Mia's hat." "Show me Leo's shoes." "Point to Mia's hand." This reinforces the vocabulary and listening comprehension.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-10 min</p></td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice speaking with correct pronunciation of /h/ (hat, hand, head) and /t/ (toes, touch) while using imperative commands ("Put on your hat!"), WH-questions ("Where is your hat?"), present simple ("This is my hand."), demonstratives (this, these), possessive adjectives (my, your), and core vocabulary (body parts, clothing, verbs like touch, wear, put on).</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: "Body Parts & Clothing Chant"</b> The teacher leads a repetitive chant. Teacher points to a body part or clothing item and says the command, students repeat and perform the action. "Touch your head! Head, head, head!" "Put on your shoes! Shoes, shoes, shoes!" (choral repetition, then individual student checks). This reinforces pronunciation and immediate response.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Drill: "What Is This/These?"</b> The teacher holds up flashcards or realia (e.g., a hat, a shoe, a picture of a hand, toes). Students use "This is my ____" or "These are my ____" to describe. Teacher: (holds up a hat) "What is this?" Student: "This is my hat." Teacher: (holds up two shoes) "What are these?" Student: "These are my shoes." (Focus on initial sounds and plural vs. singular).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction: "Ask & Point"</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of body part and clothing flashcards. Student A picks a card and asks Student B, "Where is your [body part/clothing item]?" (e.g., "Where is your ear?", "Where are your pants?"). Student B points to their own body part or clothing and responds, "This is my [ear]," or "These are my [pants]." They switch roles after a few turns. The teacher monitors for correct language use and pronunciation.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-20 min</p></td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "All About My Body".</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> "Find Your Partner!" - A collaborative guessing and describing game where students actively speak and interact.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher prepares small cards with simple descriptions of imaginary children or people, focusing on body parts and clothing (e.g., "a girl with a red hat and blue shoes," "a boy with green pants and clean hands"). Each student receives one card.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students stand up and circulate around the classroom. They must find the classmate who matches the description on their card.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">To do this, students ask questions and describe. Student A asks, "Do you have a red hat?" or "Where are your blue shoes?" Student B responds, "Yes, I have a red hat." or "These are my blue shoes." They might also ask, "What is on your head?" or "What are you wearing?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Once a student finds their matching partner, they sit down together. The game continues until all pairs are formed. This task encourages natural interaction and the use of descriptive language to locate people/objects.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p></td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist. The teacher observes students during the "Find Your Partner!" game, noting their ability to use target vocabulary, grammar, and pronunciation. A simple checklist can track participation, use of specific phrases, and clarity.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate in the "Find Your Partner!" activity, asking and responding to questions about clothing and body parts to identify their matching classmate.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Students pronounce /h/ in "hat" and /t/ in "toes" (and other words) clearly.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Students use at least 3-5 body part and 2-3 clothing words accurately (e.g., head, hand, foot, shoes, hat, pants).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and communication:</b> Students can describe features of a person or clothing clearly enough for a partner to understand (e.g., "blue hat," "red shoes").</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaborative interaction:</b> Students engage in simple exchanges, asking and responding to questions appropriately (e.g., "Do you have...?", "Yes, I do.").</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students are asked to verbally respond or show with fingers:</p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How many new body parts can you say today?" (Show with fingers 1-5).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you tell your friend about your hat or shoes?" (Thumbs up/down).</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p></td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite activity today and why?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What words about body parts or clothes did you learn or practice today?"</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The "Simon Says" game and the "Find Your Partner!" activity worked very well to engage students and encourage spontaneous speech and interaction. The use of realia and flashcards effectively supported vocabulary recall. Students showed good progress in using "This is my..." and "These are my..." sentences.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Some students still struggle with the /h/ sound initially, needing more specific drills for articulation. I will incorporate more explicit modeling and repetition for initial consonant sounds in the next lesson's warm-up. Perhaps some students need more controlled pair practice before moving to the freer production stage to build confidence.</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 4 (Writing Composition), students will apply the vocabulary and grammatical structures from today's speaking lesson to write simple sentences describing body parts and what they are wearing, moving from oral production to written expression.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p></td>
    </tr>
</table>